title: Why the GUI flickers on an Intel GPU
tags: GUI [[GUI runtime dependencies]]
kind: rationale
audience: dev
status: record
aliases: flicker flickering clignotement stale-frame webkitgtk-flicker
summary: Pieces of the window sometimes show their previous state — a row's highlight, a button, a selection — and it is WebKitGTK on the host's Intel GPU, not metafolder: a 60-line page in WebKit's own MiniBrowser does the same (October 2026).

!! The symptom

While a video plays in the [[file panel]] — a slideshow ([[Slideshow]]) or not —
pieces of the window flicker between their current and their previous state: the
[[metarecord-list panel]]'s cursor jumping between two rows (not always adjacent), a
button, a text selection, sometimes the whole window. Fast, random, and lasting until
something else is done. Moving through the files quickly is not needed; clicking a
video and selecting text with the mouse does it too.

!! Where it is not

* ''Not the page.'' A probe attached through WebKit's remote inspector (the method of [[Testing the GUI end to end]]) recorded, during the flicker, every DOM mutation in the page and every panel's shadow root, and once per frame what changes on screen without the DOM changing — the list's scroll position, the row under the pointer, the cursor row, the sizes, the focus. Over two seconds of flicker: nothing, while the screen, filmed at the same time (`ffmpeg -f x11grab`), showed a frame where the cursor sat on the previous row and the video on an older frame. What reached the screen was older than what the page held.
* ''Not metafolder.'' WebKit's own demonstration browser, `/usr/lib/webkit2gtk-4.1/MiniBrowser` (shipped with `webkit2gtk-4.1`, the engine the GUI uses), flickers the same way on a static test page: a looping `<video>`, a table with one highlighted row moved by the arrows, buttons with a `:hover` style, a paragraph to select. No Tauri, no panel, no daemon.
* ''Not DMA-BUF.'' `WEBKIT_DISABLE_DMABUF_RENDERER=1` was shipped as a fix on one quiet test and reverted when the flicker came back with it.
* ''Not a compositing window manager.'' The host runs qtile on X11, started with `startx`, with no compositor beside it.

!! Where it is

It shows on the host and never in the development container, with the same binaries
and the same WebKitGTK. The difference is the graphics stack: in the container Mesa
has no DRI3 and WebKit renders in software; on the host it renders on the GPU. The host
where it was seen:

|Graphics |Intel HD Graphics 620 (Kaby Lake GT2), kernel driver `i915`, Mesa 26.2.5 (`iris`) |
|Engine |`webkit2gtk-4.1` 2.54.2 |
|Display |X11, qtile, no compositor |

Pieces of the window showing their previous state, at the places that were just
redrawn, is what a stale tile looks like: WebKitGTK paints the page in tiles, on the
GPU through Skia since 2.46, and a tile presented before its painting is complete
shows what it held before. That is a guess the measurements fit, not a finding.

!! What was left untried

The investigation stopped once metafolder was cleared, so none of these was run. In
MiniBrowser on the test page, one variable at a time:

# `WEBKIT_SKIA_ENABLE_CPU_RENDERING=1` — the tiles painted on the CPU, the compositing and the video still on the GPU. The cheapest workaround if it is enough: the GUI could set it at startup, behind a setting, as it sets `WEBKIT_FORCE_SANDBOX` ([[Sandboxing the media decoders]]).
# `LIBGL_ALWAYS_SOFTWARE=1` — all of OpenGL in software, as in the container.
# `WEBKIT_DISABLE_COMPOSITING_MODE=1` — no accelerated compositing at all: a diagnosis, not a fix, at a cost to video.

A bug that a page this small reproduces in MiniBrowser is worth reporting upstream
(WebKit's bugzilla, component WebKitGTK), with the GPU, the driver and the versions
above.
